WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.939
If you’ve ever found yourself tossing and turning

00:00:03.939 --> 00:00:05.490
on a sweltering summer night,

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:08.730
then you know how crucial a role temperature plays

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:10.420
in a good night’s sleep.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:14.100
Under normal conditions, our body’s core temperature

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.689
drops from 98.9 down to 97.7 degrees

00:00:18.689 --> 00:00:20.000
right when we're about to fall asleep.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.039
It remains low throughout the night,

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.890
and rises again just before we awaken.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:27.360
But when environmental temperature rises,

00:00:27.369 --> 00:00:30.140
the body is prevented from shedding heat,

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.120
which results in poorer sleep.

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:34.940
So if environmental temperature can interrupt

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.270
the normal sleep cycle,

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:38.720
how might Climate Change affect our sleep?

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:41.510
--which will give us hotter nights and cause wide,

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:43.920
unexpected swings in temperature.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.960
Researchers looked at sleep data from

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:54.340
Participants were asked how many nights each month

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:56.280
they got a poor night’s sleep.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.180
The team combined these responses with data

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:00.030
from weather stations across the country.

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:02.830
They looked specifically for when nightly temperatures

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:05.600
were hotter than average from the same time period.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:08.100
According to their analysis:

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.340
changes in weather and sleep are linked.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:12.600
As temperatures rose so did

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.000
the number of restless nights.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.700
Over one month, a one degree increase

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:20.060
in nighttime temperature produced an increase of about

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:23.710
three nights of insufficient sleep per 100 people.

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:27.040
Applying this across the current population of the US

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.340
--that’s nearly:

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.020
Filtering by seasons, the effects were

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.940
tripled in the summertime.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.560
And the groups who were affected the most

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.560
were those least able to cope:

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:42.240
lower income respondents and the elderly.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:45.300
Now predicting the future is tricky

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.840
changes in technology and even human behavior

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:49.660
are difficult to account for.

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:52.560
But if trends continue, researchers predict

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:56.500
that climate change could cause in the U.S. By 2050:

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:03.020
And by 2099:

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.940
These effects may seem trivial

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.860
compared to the large scale impacts of climate change,

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:15.540
but consider how important sleep is to our well being

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.860
and that ⅓ of us already have difficulty sleeping.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.740
Even if we find ways to counter these impacts

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:23.740
with medicines or technologies,

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.480
our sleep could be very different in the future.

